---
import BaseLayout from "../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout>

  <section class="page-hero toolbox-hero">
    <div class="container">

      <div class="eyebrow">ORASOLU TOOLBOX &middot; SQL &amp; DATABASE</div>

      <h1>
        Session <span>Activity</span>
      </h1>

      <p>
        Practical Oracle SQL for reviewing active sessions, current SQL,
        wait information and blocking activity.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section resource-section">
    <div class="container">

      <div class="resource-intro">

        <div class="eyebrow">ORASOLU TOOLBOX &middot; SQL &amp; DATABASE</div>

        <h2>
          Start with the database session
        </h2>

        <p>
          When investigating database activity, the session is often a
          useful starting point. These queries provide a quick view of
          who is connected, what they are doing and whether they are
          currently waiting.
        </p>

      </div>


      <div class="resource-grid">


      <div class="resource-block">

        <div class="resource-block__header">
          <div>
            <div class="resource-block__label">01 &middot; ACTIVE SESSIONS</div>

            <h2>
              Review active database sessions
            </h2>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="resource-block__description">
          A simple starting point for seeing active user sessions and
          the SQL identifiers associated with their current activity.
        </p>

        <pre><code>SELECT
    sid,
    serial#,
    username,
    status,
    machine,
    program,
    sql_id,
    event,
    wait_class
FROM v$session
WHERE status = 'ACTIVE'
ORDER BY sid;</code></pre>

      </div>


      <div class="resource-block">

        <div class="resource-block__header">
          <div>
            <div class="resource-block__label">02 &middot; SESSION DETAILS</div>

            <h2>
              Inspect session activity
            </h2>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="resource-block__description">
          Use this view when you need more context around a particular
          session, including its user, client information, SQL and
          current wait event.
        </p>

        <pre><code>SELECT
    sid,
    serial#,
    username,
    status,
    machine,
    program,
    module,
    action,
    sql_id,
    event,
    wait_class,
    state,
    seconds_in_wait
FROM v$session
WHERE username IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY status DESC, sid;</code></pre>

      </div>


      <div class="resource-block">

        <div class="resource-block__header">
          <div>
            <div class="resource-block__label">03 &middot; WAIT INFORMATION</div>

            <h2>
              Identify sessions currently waiting
            </h2>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="resource-block__description">
          Waiting sessions can provide an important clue during
          performance investigation. Review the event together with
          the wait class and SQL identifier rather than treating the
          wait event as the complete root cause.
        </p>

        <pre><code>SELECT
    sid,
    serial#,
    username,
    sql_id,
    event,
    wait_class,
    state,
    seconds_in_wait
FROM v$session
WHERE state = 'WAITING'
ORDER BY seconds_in_wait DESC;</code></pre>

      </div>


      <div class="resource-block">

        <div class="resource-block__header">
          <div>
            <div class="resource-block__label">04 &middot; BLOCKING</div>

            <h2>
              Find blocking sessions
            </h2>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="resource-block__description">
          This query helps identify sessions that are currently
          blocking another session. Use the blocking information as
          the starting point for further investigation.
        </p>

        <pre><code>SELECT
    sid,
    serial#,
    username,
    sql_id,
    blocking_session,
    final_blocking_session,
    event,
    wait_class,
    seconds_in_wait
FROM v$session
WHERE blocking_session IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY seconds_in_wait DESC;</code></pre>

      </div></div>


      <div class="resource-note">

        <div class="eyebrow">ORASOLU TOOLBOX &middot; SQL &amp; DATABASE</div>

        <h2>
          A session query is the beginning of the investigation.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Session information is most useful when correlated with
          SQL, workload, wait events and the surrounding application
          behaviour. A single session attribute rarely explains the
          complete performance problem.
        </p>

      </div>


      <div class="resource-actions">

        <a class="button button--primary" href="/tools/sql/">
          Back to SQL & Database
        </a>

        <a class="resource-actions__link" href="/articles/oracle-wait-events/">
          Read Understanding Oracle Wait Events
        </a>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


<script>
  document.querySelectorAll(".resource-block pre").forEach((pre) => {
    const button = document.createElement("button");

    button.type = "button";
    button.className = "resource-copy-button";
    button.textContent = "Copy";
    button.setAttribute("aria-label", "Copy SQL");

    pre.style.position = "relative";

    button.style.position = "absolute";
    button.style.top = "10px";
    button.style.right = "10px";
    button.style.padding = "5px 9px";
    button.style.border = "1px solid #d1d5db";
    button.style.borderRadius = "5px";
    button.style.background = "#ffffff";
    button.style.color = "#374151";
    button.style.fontFamily = "inherit";
    button.style.fontSize = "0.75rem";
    button.style.cursor = "pointer";
    button.style.zIndex = "2";

    button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const code = pre.querySelector("code");

      if (!code) {
        return;
      }

      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(code.textContent || "");

        button.textContent = "Copied";

        window.setTimeout(() => {
          button.textContent = "Copy";
        }, 1500);
      } catch {
        button.textContent = "Copy failed";

        window.setTimeout(() => {
          button.textContent = "Copy";
        }, 1500);
      }
    });

    pre.appendChild(button);
  });
</script>
</BaseLayout>


<style>

